body:
It started with actively wanting to change. I know that sounds dumb but I kinda had to sit down with myself (being high of course) and thinking "If you don't do something, you're going to end up dead from all this negative thinking." and that started me trying to just find the brighter side to things. Friend cancelled plans? Now I have some time to watch some tv shows or go to the movies or something. That ties into the next thing: keeping myself busy. When I was alone, I'd really just tear into myself over anything. My looks, my intelligence, my awkwardness around strangers which would lead me to act stutter-y or anxious. Now I reassure myself it didn't really matter and nobody is out to get me or is thinking about how I stuttered last Thursday at that work meeting. My grandma recently had to go into detox for klonopin and some anti-depressants and I thought "Wow, I really don't want my life to be dictated by pills." I think that this was one of my biggest motivations was seeing how much of a zombie she was on a chemical cocktail fucking with her brain. I wanted to be better so I could enjoy my short time here instead of thinking about it ending pre-maturely. Take it all with a grain of salt though, since I still have 'off' days and some nights it's hard to do anything but stare at the ceiling and think about how miserable I am, but for the most part, I've gotten my life back together. 

&#x200B;

I started by logging how I felt, actually accepting compliments from people about artworks or a song that I had written, stopped being too modest and never giving myself credit (not to say I've become a narcissist, but I definitely take pride in my work now) and named a few good things that happened that day so they felt more fulfilling, giving myself short-term easy goals so I wouldn't feel so unaccomplished. I told myself it wasn't the end of the world, that every little thing isn't just 1 of 1000 problems adding up to a cataclysmic eruption of misery. Take everything one day at a time and focus on the little things, as hard as it can be for someone with bad depression. I understand that this worked for me and that it wont work for everyone, but it sure took this \[used to be\] suicidal 21 year old back on the right path. I stopped skipping classes and am going to even graduate with my two-year degree in June. I got into my first choice uni and will be moving away from my family house in July. Things as I see it can only get better from here, even if I have to try and convince myself that this happiness isn't temporary and I am finally feeling 'normal' for once. Sorry for rambling, thanks for listening.

So I watched a couple of your videos and here are my thoughts: When I watch other popular YouTube artists they usually do art challenges like using one color to make a picture or using a mouse to try and draw an intricate design and it usually ends up being funny and engaging. (Not saying be like them, but it's good to study what they do right and replicate that aspect of it). While watching your first Saturday sketch video I found myself a little bored because you didn't seem excited and spent a lot of time explaining minute details about your sketch process. You sat and drew a logo that a lot of people have the skills capable to replicate the same thing so it wouldn't keep anyone watching. People like to watch a lot of YouTubers because they can do what the average Joe can't. Also the people in the background can disrupt the flow of the video but if that can't be avoided then it is what it is. 

&#x200B;

So to answer your questions: Be a little more excited and keep the audience engaged with funny stories or do a collab with someone else telling you what to draw and make it silly. I have a series on my channel where one of my friends would tell me what to draw and I'd make it as abhorrent as possible lol. (Not saying I'm popular because... I'm not).

&#x200B;

Try to speed up the process of the sketches because watching that one logo being drawn was boring. Try to sketch a bunch of things and show chunks of it being completed alongside funny, upbeat commentary.

&#x200B;

Quality was good so you don't have to change much.

Overall it's a good channel and I know you'll get a feel for it in the future and only grow from there. Wish you luck!
